# Primary-source verification: Claude Code `allowed-tools`

Fetched 2026-09-18 from https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills
(301 redirect from https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/skills).

Quoted from the skills reference:

> The `allowed-tools` field grants permission for the listed tools during the turn
> that invokes the skill, so Claude can use them without prompting you for approval.
> The grant clears when you send your next message, even though the skill content
> stays in context; invoking the skill again re-applies it for that turn. It does not
> restrict which tools are available: every tool remains callable, and your permission
> settings still govern tools that are not listed.

> To remove tools from Claude's available pool while a skill is active, list them in
> `disallowed-tools` in the skill's frontmatter.

## Consequences for this edit

1. Absence from `allowed-tools` produces a permission prompt for an unlisted tool under
   default settings. It is not an inability to call the tool.
2. `disallowed-tools` is the restricting field. The `dev` package does not use it.
3. `disallowed-tools` is turn-scoped: the restriction clears on the user's next message,
   so it would not deliver a durable structural boundary either.
4. `references/implementation.md:13` states that "separately authorized delegation must
   preserve ownership and evidence", which presupposes delegation is possible. Adding
   `disallowed-tools: [Skill, Task]` would contradict that line.

Disposition (b) from finding F-c3d23eda is therefore rejected; disposition (a) is selected.
